  "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{2}+\\frac{5\\pi }{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\u2026,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{24}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{24}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{29\\pi }{24}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{41\\pi }{24}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{24},\\frac{17\\pi }{24},\\frac{29\\pi }{24},\\frac{41\\pi }{24}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{24}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{17\\pi }{24},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{24}\\cdot \\frac{17\\pi }{24}=\\frac{85{\\pi }^{2}}{576}.\n$$",
